<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulayhid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1047">1047</a>–<a href="/years/1138">1138</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Sulayhids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sulayhid dynasty was an Isma'ili Shia dynasty that ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from about 1047 to 1138 as allies of the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-muhammad-al-sulayhi">Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sulayhi</a> in the highlands, it ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sana'a</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibla">Dhu Jibla</a>. Its most celebrated ruler was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arwa-al-sulayhi">Queen Arwa</a> al-Sulayhi, who governed for decades and led the Isma'ili mission in Yemen.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sulayhids, the Banū Ṣulayḥ or Children of Sulayh, came from the Yemeni clan of Banu Salouh, of the al-Hajour tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashid">Hashid</a>. Isma'ili missionaries had worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> since 881, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawshab">Ibn Hawshab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-al-fadl-al-jayshani">Ali ibn al-Fadl al-Jayshani</a> arrived; Ibn al-Fadl took San'a in 905, but the Yu'firid dynasty overthrew their regime in 916.</p><p>The Fatimid mission went on despite that setback. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai">da'i</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, Sulayman az-Zawahi, befriended Ali bin Muhammad as-Sulayhi, the son of a respected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> chief from Haraz, south-west of San'a, who was nevertheless drawn to the doctrines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>. Ali converted to the Isma'ili creed in 1046 and was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">khalifa</a> within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawa">da'wa</a>. In 1047 he raised an armed force in Haraz and founded the dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ali took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabid">Zabid</a> after the Najahid ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a> was poisoned in 1060, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-dynasty">Ma'nids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a> and made them pay tribute in 1062, and in the same year conquered the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and moved north into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, for a time appointing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emirs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. He held San'a from 1063, after ending his fighting against the Zaidiyyah, and made it his capital. Relatives of the Najahids killed him, in 1067 or 1081.</p><p>Under his son al-Mukarram Ahmad the realm shrank, possibly to the area of San'a, until he rescued his captured mother, Asma bint Shihab, and won back much ground. In 1086 he handed government to his wife, Arwa, and died at the fortress of Ashyah in 1091. Arwa, recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> as suzerain over the Yemeni kings, moved her capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibla">Jibla</a> in about 1087 and ruled through strong commanders: Saba' bin Ahmad, Al-Mufaddal bin Abi'l-Barakat and Ibn Najib ad-Dawla, who attempted a coup in 1125 and was sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in a wooden cage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Throughout its existence the dynasty was an enemy of the Zaidi rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Arwa joined the Taiyabi Ismaili sect, while other Ismaili dynasties such as the Zurayids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden">Aden</a>, at first Sulayhid tributaries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> adhered to the Hafizi Ismaili sect. When Arwa died in 1138 no member of the dynasty was left, and the Sulayhid era ended.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sulayhid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
